WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.051
Some means at least one.

2
00:00:02.351 --> 00:00:04.761
Most means more than half of the named group.

3
00:00:05.061 --> 00:00:06.320
Both may include all.

4
00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:08.860
All leaves no exception within its group.

5
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.464
Most of these four volunteers are trained.

6
00:00:11.764 --> 00:00:14.367
Two would be exactly half, so it is not enough.

7
00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:16.139
Three or four meets most.

8
00:00:16.439 --> 00:00:20.343
All four could be trained; no untrained volunteer is required.

9
00:00:20.643 --> 00:00:24.205
The statement counts these volunteers, not all trained people.

10
00:00:24.505 --> 00:00:28.217
It therefore does not prove that most trained people are these volunteers.

11
00:00:28.517 --> 00:00:31.547
Quantity words determine what a statement guarantees.

12
00:00:31.847 --> 00:00:37.308
Treating some as not-all, or most as all, changes which conclusions the premises support.

13
00:00:37.608 --> 00:00:39.080
Keep its denominator.

14
00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:43.625
Ask whether the sentence gives at least one, more than half, or every member.

15
00:00:43.925 --> 00:00:47.445
Do not replace probably about one event with a headcount claim.

16
00:00:47.745 --> 00:00:50.092
Say the reference group before the quantity.

17
00:00:50.392 --> 00:00:55.917
Be careful with few and many: they depend on context and have no fixed numerical cutoff.

18
00:00:56.217 --> 00:01:00.441
Unlike most, they should not be silently converted into a precise fraction.
